Third-party material

OverTheDiff is built from Rust crates under their own licences. Cargo.lock pins every one, and
each crate's licence is in its manifest. Every crate's licence expression offers an option made only
of identifiers on the Craft licence allowlist (storytold/craft-contrib standards/licences.md), except
one MPL-2.0 crate, which that standard allows unmodified:

- option-ext 0.2.0 (MPL-2.0) by Simon Ochsenreither, through dirs-sys (the settings store's
  per-user folder lookup): used unmodified. Its source is at https://github.com/soc/option-ext and
  https://crates.io/crates/option-ext, and its licence is the crate's LICENSE.txt, which the Windows
  and Linux packages install as licences/option-ext-LICENSE.txt (on Linux, in
  /usr/share/doc/overthediff-<major>/).

Two crates also offer a GPL-family licence as an alternative, and OverTheDiff uses them under their
allowlisted options only:

- self_cell (Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-only), through epaint: used under Apache-2.0.
- r-efi (MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR LGPL-2.1-or-later), through getrandom on UEFI targets only, which
  OverTheDiff doesn't build for: used under MIT OR Apache-2.0.

Regular expressions (crates/regex) run on fancy-regex (MIT), with regex-automata and regex-syntax
(MIT OR Apache-2.0), aho-corasick and memchr (Unlicense OR MIT), and bit-set and bit-vec
(Apache-2.0 OR MIT).

On macOS and Linux, the local data source looks up owner and group names with uzers (MIT,
Copyright (c) 2019 Benjamin Sago and 2023 Sandro-Alessio Gierens), which links libc (MIT OR Apache-2.0).
It asks which file system a path is on, and whether it is mounted read-only, with rustix and
linux-raw-sys (Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT), bitflags and errno (MIT OR
Apache-2.0) on macOS and Linux, and with windows-sys and windows-link (MIT OR Apache-2.0) on Windows.
On macOS it also asks libc whether a volume's names differ by case.

The folder engine's CRC content test (otd-folder-engine) computes CRC-32 with crc32fast
(MIT OR Apache-2.0), which depends on cfg-if (MIT OR Apache-2.0).
The app's headless quick compare (`-qc`) runs the folder engine's tests on files read through the
local data source, so the otd executable includes both and the crates above; it adds no others.

The Folder Compare view (otd-ui-folder-compare) shows modified times in the local time zone with
chrono and its `clock` dependencies, as Text Compare does (below); it adds no other crates.

The launch hand-off (otd-launch) adds craft-ipc (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and its dependencies, among
them interprocess (0BSD OR Apache-2.0), recvmsg and doctest-file (0BSD), and generic-array and zmij
(MIT). The rest offer MIT OR Apache-2.0.

Commands, menus and shortcuts (otd-core, otd-ui) use craft-keymap, craft-menu and craft-quit
(MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs), with serde and serde_json, and on macOS muda (Apache-2.0
OR MIT) with keyboard-types and crossbeam-channel (MIT OR Apache-2.0) for the native menu bar.

The settings store (otd-settings) uses craft-settings (MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs),
with toml and toml_writer (MIT OR Apache-2.0), and finds the per-user folder with dirs and dirs-sys
(MIT OR Apache-2.0), redox_users (MIT) on Redox only, and option-ext (MPL-2.0, above). It writes
each saved session's file crash-safely with craft-atomic (MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs).

The path edit (otd-ui) shows the OS file and folder pickers with craft-dialogs (MIT OR Apache-2.0,
storytold/craft-libs), on rfd (MIT, Copyright (c) 2022 Bartłomiej Maryńczak), which uses pollster
0.4 (Apache-2.0 OR MIT) on Linux and the BSDs, raw-window-handle (MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR Zlib) for
the parent window, block2 (MIT) and objc2-app-kit and dispatch2 (Zlib OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT) on
macOS, and windows-sys (MIT OR Apache-2.0) on Windows.

The local data source moves items to the OS recycle bin or trash with trash (MIT, Copyright 2019
Artúr Barnabás Kovács), which brings objc2 and objc2-foundation (MIT) on macOS, the windows crates
(MIT OR Apache-2.0) on Windows, and jiff (Unlicense OR MIT) and urlencoding (MIT) on Linux. It
writes files crash-safely with craft-atomic (MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs) and asks
macOS and Linux which file system a path is on, and sets modified times there, with rustix
(Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT). On Windows it sets attribute codes with
windows-sys (MIT OR Apache-2.0).

The zip data source (otd-source) checks an archive before indexing it with craft-zip (MIT OR
Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs) and reads it with zip (MIT, Copyright (c) 2014 Mathijs van de
Nes) and its dependencies: flate2, crc32fast, indexmap, hashbrown, equivalent, memchr, typed-path,
zeroize, getrandom, thiserror, libc on Unix, windows-sys on Windows, and nix (MIT) with bitflags on
macOS and the BSDs (each MIT OR Apache-2.0 unless named); miniz_oxide (MIT OR Zlib OR Apache-2.0),
adler2 (0BSD OR MIT OR Apache-2.0), simd-adler32 (MIT), foldhash (Zlib) and constant_time_eq
(CC0-1.0 OR MIT-0 OR Apache-2.0, used under CC0-1.0 OR Apache-2.0). It turns an entry's DOS time
into local time with chrono, as Text Compare does (below).

The translation lookup (otd-core) adds craft-i18n (MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs), which
embeds CLDR number and plural-rule data, and its dependencies: the ICU4X crates (icu_*,
fixed_decimal, writeable, litemap, tinystr, potential_utf, yoke, zerofrom, zerotrie and zerovec,
with their derive crates), under Unicode-3.0, whose notice is below; synstructure (MIT); and
cobs, displaydoc, postcard, stable_deref_trait, sys-locale, utf8_iter, utf16_iter, utf_types,
write16, and windows-registry on Windows (each MIT OR Apache-2.0).
The folder comparison engine (otd-folder-engine) also uses one of those ICU4X crates,
icu_normalizer, with its Unicode normalization data, to align names across normalization forms.

Text Compare (otd-text-compare, otd-ui-text-compare) decodes legacy code-page text with
craft-codepage (MIT OR Apache-2.0, storytold/craft-libs) and the East Asian code pages with
encoding_rs ((Apache-2.0 OR MIT) AND BSD-3-Clause, copyright Mozilla Foundation; its WHATWG
Encoding Standard data's notice is below) and its dependencies cfg-if, multiversion_no_op,
scopeguard, simdutf8 and, on x86, core_detect (each MIT OR Apache-2.0), reads the Windows code page with
windows-registry (MIT OR Apache-2.0), and writes a file's modified time in the local time zone with
chrono (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and its `clock` dependencies iana-time-zone, iana-time-zone-haiku,
core-foundation-sys on macOS, windows-core on Windows and android_system_properties on Android
(each MIT OR Apache-2.0).

The tests also use egui_kittest, kittest, craft-testkit and tempfile (each MIT OR Apache-2.0),
which the app doesn't ship.

